-### F4 [medium] The design's Studio stop-and-report checks were moved to OC1, and OC1 doesn't ask their questions
-Where: f0027 Verification log ("E9's Studio confirmation ... moves to the human with OC1"); f0028 Verification log (E6); f0029 Verification log ("The E5/E6/E7 ... need Studio, so they move to the human with OC1"); f0029 OC1; raft E5, E6, E7, E9 ("Phase 1 ... A miss is stop-and-report"; "If the emulator disagrees, stop and report").
-Evidence: The raft makes four engine premises gates for execution, each with a pass/fail question and a stop-and-report fallback:
-- E9: `InputObject.Position` is in the core-UI space, so a tap on the drawn Snap frame fires snap, and a tap just right of the screen's middle looks.
-- E5: the notch and home-indicator insets come out as documented, and a 180° rotation moves LEVEL and the bar off the notch side.
-- E6: `GetMouseDelta` is non-zero during a touch.
-- E7: `bandTop` equals the top bar, and the frames share the bottom-right corner.
-
-All four were deferred. OC1's pass text is "a turn happens, the look moves ..., snap recentres, the HUD is in the margins, the radar sits above the thumb corner". A wrong E9 premise (Position in the legacy `GetGuiInset` space) moves the snap hit test by the top-bar height. On a notched landscape phone OC1's "tap snap" can still land on the overlap of the drawn and hit-tested rectangles and pass. E6 and the 180° rotation are not exercised by OC1 at all. Neither is the same-frame leak in F1.
-Critique: The deferral is reasonable for an agent with no Studio, but the stop-and-report branches the design relies on can no longer fire before merge, and OC1 as written won't trigger them afterwards.
-Consequences: Running them needs the human and Studio's device emulator, before the PR leaves draft or as a recorded acceptance of the risk. Changing the scope or wording of OC1 is the user's call.
-Recommendation: escalate to the user with these options. (a) The user runs the four premise checks in the device emulator before the PR leaves draft, using each experiment's own pass/fail question (E9's off-centre tap and right-of-middle look, E5's notched phone and 180° rotation, E6's mouse delta during a drag, E7's band top), plus F1's fast-swipe check. A miss stops the raft as the design says. (b) The user accepts merging on OC1 alone, and that acceptance is recorded as a `Decided with the user` line in each issue. Either way, put the questions in the PR description so OC1's tester sees them.
-
-### F5 [low] The snap control is drawn on desktop, where it does nothing
-Where: src/client/HudView.luau:116-127.
-Evidence: HudView always creates the translucent `Snap` circle. On a mouse-and-keyboard screen a click is not `UserInputType.Touch`, so the circle never acts. It is a new, unlabeled dead control left of the power bar on every desktop HUD. The raft says "Keyboard and mouse stay exactly as they are when no touch is down". The design says only that the frame is visible so OC1 can tap it.
-Critique: This is a visible desktop change no issue asked for. Hiding it is a product question, not a defect the author can settle alone.
-Consequences: Gating on `UserInputService.TouchEnabled` hides it on desktop but still shows it on touch laptops. Gating on `LastInputType` makes it flicker as the input changes. `HudView.snapRect` should keep returning the rectangle either way, since only touches reach the fold.
-Recommendation: escalate to the user: show it always (as now), show it only when `UserInputService.TouchEnabled`, or show it once a touch has been seen. Record the decision in f0029.
-
-### F6 [low] DangerHud's header comment exceeds the 110-column width
-Where: src/client/DangerHud.luau:3.
-Evidence: The second header line is about 150 characters long. stylua's `column_width = 110` does not wrap comments, so the gate does not catch it.
-Critique: It is clarity only.
-Consequences: none.
-Recommendation: Rewrap the comment to 110 columns.
-
-### F7 [low] A touch whose end never arrives locks its zone for the rest of the session
-Where: src/shared/Logic/Touch/init.luau (begin refuses a zone that already has a finger) and src/client/Controls.luau (the fold is never reset).
-Evidence: If an `InputEnded` for a touch is lost (for example, the app is backgrounded mid-touch; I have not verified whether Roblox guarantees an end, with `UserInputState.Cancel`, in that case), that finger stays in `fingers` for good. Every later begin in that zone is refused, so the player can never turn again (or look again), and `mouseHeld` stays set. Nothing resets the fold.
-Critique: There is no recovery path, and the failure is total for that zone. Whether it can happen depends on an unverified engine guarantee.
-Consequences: A reset (`fingers = Touch.empty()`, `ids = {}`, `onTouch(false)`) has to come from an engine event, for example `UserInputService.WindowFocusReleased`. A reset in the middle of a gesture drops that gesture, which is acceptable.
-Recommendation: Add the E-style question to F4's Studio list (background the app mid-swipe, return, and swipe again). If it fails, reset the adapter's fold on `WindowFocusReleased` in a follow-up issue.
