 ### Scalability
-- Every `store.find` reads the registry once per process (cached per directory) and checks 3 folders per root: 6 `isDir` calls instead of 3. `store.all` is unchanged in shape (one `find` per folder entry).
-- `originRegistry()` costs two git calls (`ls-tree`, `show`) per use; `publish.commit` reads it once per try with the `read` it already has.
-- `history.read`'s log has two more pathspecs; its cache key includes the roots, so a cache written for one root set is never reused for another.
-- Measured: the full gate takes 75.6 s on the prototype after design review 003 (74.7 s on the run before it) against 71.4 s on the pre-change base, 5.9% over (the same three slowest specs: c0072 land 33.0 s, c0084 spend 28.4 s, c0067 ready 26.4 s). Round 2's 81.6 s was taken while another prep's prototype gate ran on the same machine. A first run at 112.7 s was a loaded machine; a quiet rerun settled it (U1).
-
 ### Maintainability
-- The next project is one `[[project]]` entry and a folder; the next root move (c0120) is one `root` edit plus one `retired` entry, and one `paths-ignore` line in `check.yml` (the one site that can't read the registry; AC8 turns red until it is added). Nothing in the engine or the gate names a root but `projects.LEGACY`, the shapes `projects.shapeOk` accepts, repo-fence's fallback for engine-less fixtures, and the `paths-ignore` list.
-- Every reader of roots goes through `projects` (one reader per format); every reader of origin's roots through `originRegistry()`.
-- The forwarders are listed in one table (§3) and removed together.
-
 ### Usability
-- One command, `./zn`, from anywhere in the checkout (`../zn` from `z-nile/`); the directory picks the project, `--project` overrides it, and at the root a capture asks for it; `./zn find <id>` answers "where is it" across projects; `--project` is the one new flag.
-- Every refusal names the root, the project or the registry rule it broke.
-- `.tracker/` is hidden: the tracker skill says `rg --hidden`, `./zn find` or `git grep`.
 
