 ### Scalability
-- `ac.sh` adds one `lune run z-nile/roots` (about 60 ms) per `check.sh`.
-- `graph.aliases` reads at most one `.luaurc` per ancestor folder, cached per absolute folder.
-- The gate's spec runs are one per home, serial locally; CI runs the projects in parallel jobs. Gate time is measured against the base in the Verification log (E2).
-- `busy` runs one `git diff` per branch and one `git status` per worktree: about 1 s here.
-
 ### Maintainability
-- A new game is a folder `<name>/` with `.tracker/`, a `[[project]]` entry, `tests/run.luau` (a one-line `require`), `.luaurc`, `check.sh` and its forwarders, plus a CI matrix entry and a required check. The gate, the selector, `ac.sh`, CI's dispatch step, promotion, compaction, spend and the old-roots checks all read the registry.
-- One shell reader of the registry (`z-nile/roots`), one Luau reader (`projects.luau`).
-- The forwarders in each home's `scripts/` go with c0114's forwarder-removal raft.
-
 ### Usability
-- From `pickman/`, everything a pickman developer types works as before: `lune run tests/run -- tests/unit/Maze`, `./check.sh`, `rojo serve`, `scripts/tracker.sh find f0104`. From `z-nile/`, `lune run tests/run -- tests/tooling/…` and `./check.sh` (every project) or `scripts/check.sh` (z-nile's). `./zn find` answers relative to where you are.
-- `lune run tests/run` at the root refuses with a message naming the homes.
-- `z-nile/check.sh --plan` says what a gate run would read, and `./zn busy` says what blocks a move.
 
